==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ei,ej,ey<id:interjection>,eye}}, ultimately {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}} of the natural utterance, although probably also influenced by {{cog|xno,fro|ahi}}, {{cog|fro|haï}}, and {{cog|frm|aï,aïe,ay}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=int|id=6007812391}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/aɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ay.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|aye|ay|ey<id:island><t:island>|eye|I}}

====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# [[ah|Ah]]! [[alas]]! {{n-g|Expressing [[anger]], [[alarm]], [[frustration]], [[pain]], etc.}}
#: {{alti|en|aie|aye}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1559|author=[[w:Seneca the Younger|Lucius, Anneus, Seneca]]|chapter_tlr=[[w:Jasper Heywood|Iasper Heywood]]|chapter=The Preface to the Tragedye|title=The Sixt Tragedie of the Most Graue and Prudent Author Lucius, Anneus, Seneca, Entituled [[w:Troades (Seneca)|Troas]],{{nb...|with Diuers and Sundrye Addicions to the Same. Newly Set Forth in Englishe by Iasper Heywood Studient in Oxenforde.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Imprinted at London in Fletestrete within Temple barre, at the signe of the hand and starre, by}} [[w:Richard Tottel|Richard Tottyll]]|section=signature &#91;A5&#93;, verso|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_the-sixt-tragedie-of-the_seneca-lucius-annaeus_1559/page/n9/mode/1up|oclc=751825204|passage=And ſuch as yet, coulde neuer weapon wꝛeſt, / But on the lappe are woont to dandled be, / Ne yet foꝛgotten had the mothers bꝛeſt, / How greekes them ſlew, alas here ſhall ye ſe, / To make repoꝛte therof, '''ay''' woe is me, / My ſong is miſchiefe, murder miſerye.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|Northern England|and|Scotland}}<!--OED--> {{n-g|Expressing [[earnestness]], [[surprise]], [[wonder]], etc.}}
#* {{RQ:New English Dictionary|year=1863|author=Mrs. Toogood|title=Specimens of the Yorkshire Dialect|volume2=I|entry2=Ay (''ē''ⁱ)|pos=int|page2=601|column2=2|passage='''Ay''' my word! I am glad to see you.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Jekyll and Hyde|page=137|passage=Under the strain of this continually impending doom and by the sleeplessness to which I now condemned myself, '''ay''', even beyond what I had thought possible to man, I became, in my own person, a creature eaten up and emptied by fever, languidly weak both in body and mind, and solely occupied by one thought: the horror of my other self.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=9 December 1888|author=Æthelbert {{quote-gloss|Binns}}|title=Thoughts in Verse. On Finding a Buttercup.|journal=[[w:Keighley News|The Keighley News]]|location=Keighley, West Yorkshire|date_published=16 March 1889|page=7|pageurl=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002011/18890316/083/0007|column=7|oclc=18555130|passage='''Ay'''! bonny little buttercup, what are ta dewin’ heear, / Hoddin’ up thi tiny heead, this raw, cowd time o’ t’year?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Cynthia B. Huntington|chapter=Full Circle|title=Through Her Eyes: An Infidel’s Perspective{{nb...|A Western Woman’s Adventures in an Ancient Ottoman Culture}}|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|page=88|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/throughhereyes0000hunt/page/88/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4568-5098-2|passage='''Ay''', lass, you’ve ruined your chances now. When you left for New York to become a Pan Am stewardess we thought you’d got it made.}}
# {{n-g|Used in {{m|en|aye aye|ay, ay}}.}}

=====Derived terms=====
